# Lesson 4: Machine Learning

> OBJ:
  - Define Machine Learning (ML) and explain its relationship with AI.
  - Explain the basic principles of Machine Learning.
  - Build and test simple Machine Learning models using Teachable Machine.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room with webcams or phones)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 6 min | Show 10 mango vs 10 orange photos, then a new fruit: "How did you know?" – learning from examples. | Explain. |
| Core 1 | 18 min | Define ML; AI ⊃ ML ⊃ deep learning (Venn); traditional programming (rules + data → answers) vs ML (data + answers → rules/model); types (supervised: classification/regression; unsupervised: clustering; reinforcement). | Notes; Venn. |
| Core 2 | 26 min | Principles: data (features, labels), training/validation/test split, model and parameters, loss/error, optimisation (gradient descent intuition), generalisation, overfitting/underfitting, evaluation metrics (accuracy, precision/recall, confusion matrix), bias in data, iteration; ML pipeline. | Paper "train a classifier" activity. |
| Core 3 | 42 min | **Teachable Machine** (teachablemachine.withgoogle.com): image project (e.g., "pen / eraser / hand" or "healthy vs diseased leaf" photos), classes, capture 30–50 samples each, train, preview, test with new items; audio project (clap vs whistle); pose project; export (TensorFlow.js/Lite) and embed. Experiments: fewer samples, one-sided lighting, confusing background – observe accuracy; confusion matrix in "Under the hood". No-webcam alternative: upload phone photos. | Build, test and improve two models; record accuracy. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket; discuss bias observed. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 4.1 Machine learning and its relationship with AI

> DEF:
  **Machine learning (ML)** is a subset of AI in which systems **learn patterns from data** to make predictions or decisions **without being explicitly programmed** for each rule. **Deep learning** is a subset of ML using multi-layer neural networks. AI is the broad goal; ML is the dominant technique for achieving it today.

| Approach | Input | Output |
| Traditional programming | Rules (program) + data | Answers |
| Machine learning | Data + answers (labels) | Rules (model) → used to answer new data |

### 4.2 Basic principles

| Principle | Explanation |
| **Data: features and labels** | Each example is described by **features** (pixel values, age, income, words); in supervised learning it has a **label** (cat/dog, price, spam/not). Quality and representativeness of data determine the model – "garbage in, garbage out" |
| **Types of learning** | **Supervised** – learn mapping from features to labels: **classification** (discrete classes) and **regression** (numbers). **Unsupervised** – find structure without labels: **clustering**, dimensionality reduction, anomaly detection. **Reinforcement** – agent learns actions from rewards |
| **Model and parameters** | A model is a function with adjustable **parameters** (weights); training adjusts them. Model families: linear/logistic regression, decision trees, k-nearest neighbours, support vector machines, neural networks |
| **Training** | Show examples repeatedly (**epochs**); measure the **loss/error** between predictions and labels; adjust weights to reduce loss (**gradient descent** – step downhill on the error surface; **learning rate** = step size) |
| **Train / validation / test split** | Train on ~70–80 %, tune on validation, evaluate on unseen **test** data – measures **generalisation** |
| **Overfitting vs underfitting** | Overfitting: memorises training data (high train accuracy, poor test accuracy) – fix with more/diverse data, simpler model, regularisation. Underfitting: too simple to capture the pattern – use richer features or model |
| **Evaluation metrics** | **Accuracy** (correct/total); **confusion matrix** (true/false positives/negatives); **precision** (of predicted positives, how many correct) and **recall** (of actual positives, how many found) – vital when classes are imbalanced (disease detection); for regression, mean error |
| **Bias and fairness** | Unbalanced or unrepresentative data → biased predictions (e.g., only light-skin faces, only one dialect); check class balance and test across groups |
| **Iteration and deployment** | Collect → prepare → train → evaluate → improve → deploy → monitor (data drift) |
| **Transfer learning** | Reusing a large pre-trained model and retraining only the last layers on a small dataset – how Teachable Machine trains in seconds |

### 4.3 Building and testing with Teachable Machine

| Step | Image project (e.g. "Healthy leaf / Diseased leaf / No leaf") |
| 1 | Open teachablemachine.withgoogle.com ▸ Get Started ▸ **Image Project ▸ Standard** |
| 2 | Rename Class 1/2, add Class 3; for each class **Webcam ▸ Hold to Record** 40–60 samples varying angle, distance, background and lighting (or **Upload** phone photos) |
| 3 | **Train Model** (default: 50 epochs, batch 16, learning rate 0.001 – Advanced shows them); training is transfer learning on MobileNet in the browser – no data leaves the device |
| 4 | **Preview**: show new items; watch class probability bars; test edge cases (partly hidden leaf, different leaf) |
| 5 | **Advanced ▸ Under the hood**: accuracy per class, **confusion matrix**, loss curve – identify weak classes |
| 6 | Improve: add samples for confused classes, balance counts, diversify backgrounds, remove mislabelled samples; retrain and compare |
| 7 | **Export Model** ▸ TensorFlow.js (upload to shareable link) or download; embed snippet in a web page (Lesson 46 link); Lite for phones/Raspberry Pi |
| Variants | **Audio project** (background noise + "clap" + "whistle", 20 one-second samples each); **Pose project** ("sitting straight" vs "slouching" – ergonomics reminder) |

Experiments to teach principles: (a) train with 5 vs 50 samples per class → underfitting/generalisation; (b) record one class only in bright light → the model learns lighting, not the object (**spurious correlation/bias**); (c) unbalanced classes (60 vs 10) → biased predictions and misleading accuracy – read precision/recall; (d) test with a person/leaf not in training → generalisation; (e) change epochs/learning rate → training dynamics.

> EXAMPLE:
  **From Teachable Machine to a farm tool.** Learners in Foumbot train a three-class model on 150 phone photos of tomato leaves (healthy, early blight, leaf curl). First accuracy 92 % – but the confusion matrix shows leaf curl confused with healthy when photographed indoors; adding 40 outdoor samples raises recall for leaf curl from 70 % to 90 %. They export to TensorFlow Lite, embed it in a simple web page and the agriculture club uses it during a field visit – noting it is an aid, not a diagnosis, and that the dataset covers only local varieties.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 4.1 – AI ⊃ ML ⊃ Deep learning Venn diagram** with example systems in each ring. **Figure 4.2 – ML pipeline**: data collection → labelling → split → training (loss curve descending) → evaluation (confusion matrix) → deployment → monitoring loop. **Figure 4.3 – Overfitting vs underfitting**: three plots (too simple line, good fit, wiggly memorising curve) with train/test accuracy bars. **Figure 4.4 – Teachable Machine screen**: three class panels with sample thumbnails, Train button, Preview probability bars.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "High training accuracy means a good model." Only accuracy on **unseen** test data counts. "More data always fixes it." More of the *same biased* data reinforces bias – diversity matters. "The model understands leaves." It correlates pixel patterns; it can latch onto backgrounds or lighting.

> ACTIVITY:
  **ML lab (50 min, pairs).** (1) Paper warm-up: classify 12 "fruit" cards by two features on a grid; draw a decision boundary; test with 3 new cards. (2) Teachable Machine image model with three classes; record accuracy and confusion matrix before/after improvement; document the bias experiment (lighting). (3) Audio or pose model of choice. (4) Export one model and open the generated web page. (5) Reflection sheet: features, labels, classes, samples per class, epochs, accuracy, weakest class, cause, fix, one ethical note (whose images, consent). No-webcam alternative: teacher-provided photo sets uploaded from a shared folder.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Define machine learning and explain its relationship with AI and deep learning using a diagram.
  2. Contrast traditional programming with machine learning.
  3. Explain features, labels, training, testing and generalisation.
  4. What are overfitting and underfitting? How would you detect and fix overfitting?
  5. Interpret a confusion matrix (given) and compute accuracy, precision and recall for one class.
  6. Describe the steps to build, test and improve an image model in Teachable Machine and one experiment that reveals data bias.
  **Homework:** Build (or plan on paper if no device) a Teachable Machine model solving a small local problem (sorting waste types, recognising school uniforms vs not, coins); report data, results and limits in half a page.

