# Lesson 5: Developing AI Systems

> OBJ:
  - Describe the major stages involved in developing an AI system.
  - Identify programming languages and tools used in AI development.
  - Explain why Python is widely used in AI development.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room preferable)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 6 min | "You have been asked to build an AI that predicts which learners need extra maths help. Where do you start?" | Brainstorm steps. |
| Core 1 | 30 min | Stages: problem definition and feasibility; data collection; data preparation (cleaning, labelling, splitting); model selection; training; evaluation; tuning; deployment (app, API, edge); monitoring and maintenance; ethics/governance throughout (CRISP-DM / ML lifecycle). Roles (data scientist, ML engineer, domain expert). | Map the maths-help project onto stages. |
| Core 2 | 24 min | Languages and tools: Python (NumPy, pandas, scikit-learn, TensorFlow, PyTorch, Keras, OpenCV, NLTK/spaCy, Hugging Face), R, Java, C++, Julia, JavaScript (TensorFlow.js), MATLAB, Prolog/Lisp (historic); environments: Jupyter/Colab, VS Code, Anaconda; data tools (SQL, Excel), cloud (Google Cloud AI, AWS, Azure), AutoML, no-code (Teachable Machine, Orange, KNIME), APIs (OpenAI, Gemini), hardware (GPUs/TPUs, Raspberry Pi/Arduino for edge). | Tool taxonomy. |
| Core 3 | 32 min | Why Python: simple readable syntax, huge AI/data libraries, community, glue language over fast C/C++ backends, notebooks, portability, free and open source, industry/academia standard; demo: 12-line scikit-learn classifier in Colab/offline Python (Iris or a marks dataset) with train/test split and accuracy; read and annotate the code. | Run/annotate script; modify test size. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 5.1 Major stages of developing an AI system

| Stage | Key activities | Outputs | Pitfalls |
| **1. Problem definition & feasibility** | Define the goal and success metric; is AI the right tool? stakeholders, constraints (data, budget, ethics, law), risk classification | Project brief, metric (e.g. recall ≥ 0.9) | Vague goals; solving the wrong problem |
| **2. Data collection** | Identify sources (databases, sensors, surveys, public datasets, web); obtain consent/licences; volume and representativeness | Raw dataset, data inventory | Bias, privacy violations, insufficient data |
| **3. Data preparation** | Clean (missing values, duplicates, errors), label/annotate, transform (normalise, encode), feature engineering, split train/validation/test, document (datasheets) | Clean labelled dataset | 60–80 % of effort; leakage between splits |
| **4. Model selection** | Choose approach (rules, classical ML, deep learning, pre-trained model/LLM), baseline first, consider explainability and compute | Candidate models | Over-complex choices |
| **5. Training** | Fit the model; hyper-parameters (epochs, learning rate); hardware (CPU/GPU/cloud); experiment tracking | Trained model | Overfitting; unreproducible runs |
| **6. Evaluation** | Test on unseen data; metrics; fairness across groups; error analysis; compare to baseline and human performance | Evaluation report | Testing on training data |
| **7. Tuning & iteration** | Adjust data, features, model, hyper-parameters; repeat 3–6 | Improved model | Endless tuning without value |
| **8. Deployment** | Integrate into product: web/mobile app, API/cloud service, embedded/edge device; user interface; documentation; security | Running system | Ignoring users; latency; cost |
| **9. Monitoring & maintenance** | Track performance and **data drift**, feedback loops, retraining schedule, incident response, updates | Dashboards, retrained versions | "Set and forget" |
| **Cross-cutting: ethics & governance** | Privacy, consent, bias audits, transparency, human oversight, compliance (Law 2024/017), impact assessment | Ethics review, model card | Treated as afterthought |

### 5.2 Programming languages and tools

| Category | Examples | Role |
| **Languages** | **Python** (dominant), **R** (statistics), **Java/Scala** (enterprise, big data – Spark), **C++** (performance, robotics, game AI, model backends), **Julia** (scientific), **JavaScript/TypeScript** (TensorFlow.js in browsers), **MATLAB** (engineering), **Prolog/Lisp** (classic symbolic AI), SQL (data) | Writing models, pipelines and applications |
| **Python libraries** | **NumPy** (arrays), **pandas** (tables), **Matplotlib/Seaborn** (plots), **scikit-learn** (classical ML), **TensorFlow/Keras** and **PyTorch** (deep learning), **OpenCV** (vision), **NLTK/spaCy/Hugging Face Transformers** (NLP), XGBoost | Ready-made algorithms |
| **Environments** | **Jupyter Notebook**, **Google Colab** (free cloud GPUs), VS Code, PyCharm, Anaconda | Interactive coding and documentation |
| **Data & big-data tools** | Excel/LibreOffice Calc, SQL databases, Apache Spark, Hadoop, Kaggle datasets, Label Studio (annotation) | Storing, labelling, processing data |
| **Cloud AI platforms & APIs** | Google Vertex AI, AWS SageMaker, Azure ML, OpenAI/Gemini/Claude APIs, Hugging Face Hub | Training at scale, hosted models |
| **No-code / low-code & AutoML** | **Teachable Machine**, Orange, KNIME, Google AutoML, Lobe | Prototyping without programming |
| **Hardware** | GPUs (NVIDIA), TPUs, edge devices (Raspberry Pi, Arduino with TinyML, Jetson), smartphones (NPUs) | Training and deployment |
| **MLOps & deployment** | Git/GitHub, Docker, Flask/FastAPI, TensorFlow Lite, ONNX, MLflow | Versioning, serving, monitoring |

### 5.3 Why Python dominates AI development

| Reason | Explanation |
| **Simple, readable syntax** | Close to pseudocode; researchers and students focus on ideas, not boilerplate; fast prototyping |
| **Rich ecosystem of libraries** | Almost every algorithm available: scikit-learn, TensorFlow, PyTorch, pandas, OpenCV – tested and documented |
| **Performance through "glue"** | Heavy computation runs in optimised C/C++/CUDA underneath; Python orchestrates – best of both |
| **Community and learning resources** | Largest AI community, tutorials, Kaggle notebooks, Stack Overflow, free courses |
| **Notebooks and interactivity** | Jupyter/Colab support experimentation, visualisation and sharing |
| **Free, open source, cross-platform** | Runs on Windows, Linux, macOS, Raspberry Pi; no licence cost – important for Cameroonian schools and start-ups |
| **Integration** | Connects to web frameworks (Flask, Django), databases, cloud APIs, IoT devices |
| **Industry and academic standard** | Used by Google, Meta, OpenAI, universities; most job postings and papers |

```
# Minimal supervised ML in Python (scikit-learn): predict Pass/Fail from study hours & attendance
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score

X = [[2,60],[8,95],[5,80],[1,50],[9,98],[4,70],[7,90],[3,65],[6,85],[2,55]]   # features: hours/week, attendance %
y = ["Fail","Pass","Pass","Fail","Pass","Fail","Pass","Fail","Pass","Fail"]  # labels

X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.3, random_state=1)
model = DecisionTreeClassifier().fit(X_train, y_train)      # training
print("Accuracy:", accuracy_score(y_test, model.predict(X_test)))  # evaluation
print("New learner 5 h, 75%:", model.predict([[5,75]]))      # deployment-style prediction
```

> EXAMPLE:
  **Building "MoMo Guard" (a fraud-detection AI) – stages in action.** Problem: detect fraudulent transfers with recall ≥ 95 % and few false alarms. Data: 2 million anonymised transactions with fraud labels (consent via terms; Law 2024/017 compliance). Preparation: remove duplicates, engineer features (amount vs history, time, new device), split by time. Model: gradient-boosted trees (explainable enough) baseline, then a neural net. Training on cloud GPUs; evaluation shows bias against rural users with irregular patterns → threshold adjusted per segment. Deployment as an API scoring transactions in 50 ms; monitoring detects drift when new scam types appear → monthly retraining. Tools: Python, pandas, scikit-learn, XGBoost, PyTorch, MLflow, FastAPI, Docker.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 5.1 – AI development lifecycle** as a loop of nine stages with an "ethics & governance" ring around it and typical tools under each stage. **Figure 5.2 – Tool stack pyramid**: hardware → languages → libraries → environments → platforms/APIs → applications. **Figure 5.3 – Annotated Python script** with callouts: import, features/labels, split, train, evaluate, predict.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "AI development is mostly coding models." Most time goes to problem framing, data work and deployment/monitoring. "Python is slow so unsuitable." The heavy lifting runs in compiled backends; Python's productivity wins.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Plan and prototype (50 min).** (1) Groups map a chosen problem (predicting learners at risk; classifying market produce; triaging clinic messages) onto the nine stages with concrete data sources, metrics, tools and two ethical safeguards – present as a one-page project canvas. (2) Individually run the scikit-learn script (Colab, local Python, or paper trace if no computer): annotate each line, change test_size to 0.5, add a new learner, note the accuracy change and why it is unreliable with 10 rows. (3) Tool hunt: match 12 tool cards to stages.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Describe the major stages of developing an AI system and one pitfall at each of four stages.
  2. Why does data preparation consume most of the effort? Give three preparation tasks.
  3. Identify six languages or tools used in AI development and their roles.
  4. Distinguish scikit-learn, TensorFlow/PyTorch and Teachable Machine by purpose and user.
  5. Explain five reasons why Python is widely used in AI development.
  6. In the sample script, identify the features, labels, training step and evaluation step.
  **Homework:** Produce an "AI project canvas" for a community problem: goal and metric, data (source, consent), preparation, model type, tools (with Python libraries), deployment target, monitoring plan, ethical safeguards.

